from .common import pick, truth, written

NOTES = {
    1: ['The deck climbs the layers: the **hub** (layer 1, repeats bits), the **link-layer switch** (layer 2, forwards frames by MAC address and learns where hosts are), and the **router** (layer 3, forwards packets by IP address). Along the way it explains **collision domains** and **broadcast domains**, the **loop problem** in switched networks and the **spanning tree** fix, and finally **VLANs**, which create broadcast domains in software. Counting domains in a diagram is the classic exam question, so practise it on slides 23, 24, 27 and 28.'],
    2: ['Hosts and networks rarely work alone. **Connecting devices** link hosts together into a network, or link networks together into an internet. Because they work at different layers of the model, they differ in what they can read (bits, MAC addresses or IP addresses) and so in how cleverly they forward traffic. The three covered here are **hubs**, **link-layer switches** and **routers**.'],
    3: ['Each device covers the layers up to the one it works at. A **hub** works only at the **physical** layer. A **link-layer switch** works at the **physical and data link** layers. A **router** works up to the **network** layer. The higher a device goes, the more of each unit of data it understands, and the smarter its forwarding decisions.'],
    4: ['A **hub** is a **physical-layer device**: it works on **bits**, not frames, and doesn’t understand addresses at all. Stations plug into it in a **physical star**, but because it copies every signal to everyone, it behaves **logically like a bus**: one shared medium.',
        'Each station’s connection to the hub uses **two pairs** of twisted-pair wire: one pair for **transmitting** and one for **receiving**.',
        'When a bit arrives on one port, the hub sends it out of **all the other ports**, **without any access method** of its own and **without filtering**. Filtering would mean reading the frame’s destination MAC address and choosing the right port; a hub can’t do that. So every station receives every frame, and if two send at once their signals collide.',
        'Hubs can be chained to extend a network. In a star, the cable from a host’s NIC to the hub can be at most **100 m**, so two hosts on the same hub can be at most **200 m** apart, which is the maximum network length.'],
    5: ['A sends a frame for B. The hub repeats it to **B, C and D**. Each NIC checks the destination MAC address in the frame: **B** sees its own address and **keeps** the frame; **C and D** see someone else’s address and **discard** it. The filtering happens in the NICs, not in the hub, and the frame still used up everyone’s link.'],
    6: ['A hub, with everything connected to it, forms **a single collision domain** and **a single broadcast domain**.',
        'A **collision domain** is the part of the network (the set of devices) in which, if **two or more nodes transmit** at the same time, their frames **collide**. On a hub, any two stations can collide, so the whole hub is one collision domain.',
        'A **broadcast domain** is the part of the network in which every device **receives** the other devices’ **broadcast** messages. A hub repeats broadcasts to everyone, so the whole hub is one broadcast domain too. Simple way to tell them apart: collision = who can crash into each other when **sending**; broadcast = who **hears** each other’s broadcasts.'],
    7: ['Joining LAN segments with hubs has one **advantage**: it **extends the maximum distance** between nodes, since each hub adds another 100 m of reach.',
        'The **disadvantage**: the separate segments’ collision domains merge into **one large collision domain**. Now a station in one department can collide with a station in another, and every station shares the same bandwidth, so **performance drops** as the network grows.'],
    8: ['A **link-layer switch** is a **data link layer** device. It also does physical-layer work: it **regenerates** the signal it receives, like a hub.',
        'The difference from a hub is that, as a data link device, the switch can **read the MAC addresses** (source and destination) in each frame, and use them to decide where the frame should go.'],
    9: ['A switch is a data link device that deals with **frames**. It is used to **divide** (segment) a LAN into smaller segments.',
        'Where a medium is still shared (a bus or a hub on one of its ports), the switch runs CSMA/CD before sending, so **each switch port is a separate collision domain**: a collision on one port doesn’t affect any other. And it **filters**: instead of copying a frame to every port, it **forwards** it only out of the port that leads to the **destination MAC address**.',
        'To know which port leads where, the switch keeps a **forwarding table** (switch table, MAC table). The table starts **empty** and fills **automatically** by **learning** from the frames passing through: nobody configures it. Each entry holds a host’s **MAC address**, the **switch interface** it is connected to, and the **time** the entry was created (old entries expire so the table stays current when hosts move).'],
    10: ['The two pictures side by side. A **hub** receiving a packet from F sends it out of **every** port. A **switch** receiving the same packet sends it **only to C**, the destination. The other links stay free for other traffic, which is why a switch improves performance and privacy.'],
    11: ['The switching table is just a list of **MAC address → port**. Here four hosts are listed, each against the port (1 to 4) it is connected to. When a frame arrives, the switch looks up its destination MAC in this table to find the outgoing port.'],
    12: ['**Learning** uses the **source** address. When a frame arrives, the switch looks up the frame’s **source MAC** in the table. If it isn’t there, the switch **adds** it with the **interface the frame came in on**: now it knows where that host is.',
         'If the source MAC is already there, the switch **updates** the interface if it has changed (the host moved to another port) and refreshes the record time.',
         '**Forwarding** uses the **destination** address. If the destination MAC is in the table, the switch compares its stored interface with the one the frame arrived on: if they are **different**, it **forwards** the frame out of the stored interface only; if they are the **same**, the destination is on the segment the frame came from, so the switch **drops** it (it has already arrived).',
         'If the destination is **not** in the table, the switch **floods** the frame out of **every interface except the one it came in on**, so it reaches the destination wherever it is. **Broadcast and multicast** frames are always sent out of all ports. Memory aid: the source teaches, the destination decides.'],
    13: ['Follow the figure step by step. (a) The table is **empty**. (b) A sends to D: the switch **learns** “A is on port 1”; D is unknown, so it **floods** to every other port. (c) D sends to B: it learns “D is on port 4”; B is unknown, so it floods. (d) B sends to A: it learns B’s port; A is known, so it forwards **only to port 1**. (e) C sends to D: it learns C’s port; D is known, so it forwards only to port 4. After a few frames the table is complete and flooding stops.'],
    14: ['In a **switched network**, several switches connect the different parts of a LAN. For **reliability**, designers add **redundant** switches and links, so that if one link fails, another path still connects the hosts.',
         'The side effect: two hosts are now connected by **more than one path**, which forms a **loop**. Switches, unlike routers, have no way to notice that a frame has already been around the loop, so loops cause serious trouble (next slide).'],
    15: ['Loops cause a **broadcast storm**. A switch floods **broadcast** frames (destination FF-FF-FF-FF-FF-FF), **multicast** frames and **unknown unicast** frames (destination not in its table) out of **all ports except the one they came from**.',
         'With a loop, each switch that receives the flooded frame floods it again, so copies travel around the loop **forever** and **multiply** at every switch. There is nothing like a time-to-live in an Ethernet frame to stop them. Within seconds the copies use up all the bandwidth and the switches’ processing, and the **network stops**. That is a broadcast storm.',
         'The fix is the **spanning tree protocol** (STP), which removes the loops logically while keeping the redundant links available as backups.'],
    16: ['A Packet Tracer simulation of a storm: a single broadcast from PC1 enters a triangle of three switches, and each switch keeps passing copies to the others. The same frame circles round and round, and more copies are made at every step.'],
    17: ['The idea comes from graph theory: for any connected graph there is a **spanning tree**, a subset of the links that still **connects every node** but has **no closed loops**. STP finds such a tree for the switches.',
         'STP removes the loops **logically**, not physically: no cable is unplugged.',
         'It does this by **blocking** certain ports. A **blocked port** doesn’t forward ordinary data frames, but it still passes the **spanning tree protocol’s own frames**, so the switches keep monitoring the network. If something changes (a link fails), STP runs again and a blocked port can **become a forwarding port**, so the redundant link takes over as a backup.'],
    18: ['The example network: five switches, S1 to S5, joining four LANs, with redundant paths between them. As a graph, the switches and LANs are nodes and each connection is an edge. Because there are several paths between LANs, there are loops that STP has to break.'],
    19: ['How the spanning tree is found: first one switch is chosen as the **root** (here S1). Then, for every other switch, STP keeps only the **shortest path to the root** (costs of 1 from a switch to a LAN and 0 from a LAN to a switch). The links that are on some shortest path are kept; the rest are blocked.'],
    20: ['The result: **ports 2 and 3 of S3** and **port 1 of S5** become **blocking ports**. Every LAN can still reach every other LAN, but there is now only one active path between any two of them, so no loop remains. Data frames never leave through a blocked port.'],
    21: ['Where the medium is shared, a switch implements CSMA/CD. It **isolates collision domains**: each segment (port) is its own collision domain, so there are fewer collisions and a **higher total throughput**, because several pairs of hosts can communicate at the same time.',
         'However, a switch still forwards a **broadcast** frame to **every** device on the whole switched network: a switched network without VLANs is a **single broadcast domain**. Only routers (or VLANs) split broadcast domains.',
         'More advantages: better **performance and security**, because a frame only goes out of the port leading to its destination; **wider coverage**, with no limit on the size of LANs joined by switches; and it is **transparent** and **plug-and-play**: installing or removing a switch, or moving a host, doesn’t require reconfiguring anything.'],
    22: ['Every switch port is its own collision domain. The lower part of the figure adds full duplex: with separate transmit and receive paths on each link, there are **no collisions at all**, so CSMA/CD isn’t needed.'],
    23: ['The network built with **hubs only** (three hubs, four PCs) is **one collision domain** and **one broadcast domain**. If the bandwidth is 100 Mbps, the four hosts share it, so each gets an average maximum effective bandwidth of **100/4 = 25 Mbps**.'],
    24: ['The same network built with **switches**: count the **links** between a switch port and another device. There are **6** (four PC links and two switch-to-switch links), so **6 collision domains**. With no router, it is still **1 broadcast domain**.',
         'Each host now has its own link, and in full duplex it can send and receive at once, so each gets up to **200 Mbps** (100 each way) instead of 25 Mbps, and CSMA/CD isn’t needed.'],
    25: ['Switches can work in **full-duplex** mode: sending and receiving frames at the same time over the same interface.',
         'They do MAC address recognition and frame forwarding in **hardware** (special chips), which is why they are fast.',
         'There are two types. **Store-and-forward**: the switch receives the **whole frame**, buffers it briefly, **checks it for errors** (the CRC), and only then sends it to the right output. That is reliable, but the buffering adds **delay**.',
         '**Cut-through**: since the destination address is at the **start** of the frame, the switch starts sending the frame to the output port as soon as it has read that address (if the output is free), **without buffering** the rest. That means no buffering delay, but **no error checking**: a corrupted frame is forwarded anyway. Memory aid: store-and-forward = safe but slow; cut-through = quick but careless.'],
    26: ['A **router** works at the **network layer**, so it deals with **packets**, not frames. It connects LANs and WANs, even ones using different protocols. Each router interface has both a **physical (MAC) address** and an **IP address**.',
         'Routers **isolate both collision domains and broadcast domains**: a router doesn’t forward broadcasts from one interface to another, so **each router interface is a separate broadcast domain**. A router behaves like a normal station on each network it joins, but it has more than one logical (IP) address, one per interface.',
         'It forwards packets based on the **destination IP address**, exchanges routing information with other routers, and uses a routing algorithm to find the **best route**. If it has no route to the destination, it **discards** the packet, unlike a switch, which floods frames it doesn’t know where to send.'],
    27: ['Counting domains in this example. **Broadcast domains** = the router interfaces in use: **2** (the LAN side and the Internet side). **Collision domains** = every link that starts at a switch or router port: **9**. Method: count each cable that ends at a switch or router port; a hub and everything attached to it count as one.'],
    28: ['The organisation LAN has **4 broadcast domains**, one for each router interface with a network behind it (including the one served by the Wi-Fi router). The counting rules: every **switch or router port** starts a new collision domain (a hub port doesn’t); every **router port** starts a new broadcast domain (switch and hub ports don’t, unless VLANs are used).'],
    29: ['The switch vs. router table, point by point. **Layer**: router 3 (packets), switch 2 (frames). **Addresses**: router interfaces have an IP and a MAC; switch interfaces have a MAC. **Domains**: router isolates broadcast and collision domains; switch isolates only collision domains. **Unknown destination**: router **drops** the packet; switch **floods** the frame. **Table**: router uses a routing table; switch uses a MAC table. **Setup**: router needs configuration; switch is plug-and-play. **Cut-through**: switches can use it; routers don’t. This is the most likely written comparison in the chapter.'],
    30: ['The network here has **12 collision domains** and **3 broadcast domains** (three subnets, each on its own switch, joined by a router). Now suppose a new user belongs to subnet 1, but there is no space in subnet 1’s room, only in the room where subnet 3 is. To keep the user in subnet 1, you would have to run a cable from subnet 1’s switch to the other room, which is hard and costly.',
         'The alternative is a **VLAN** (virtual, or logical, LAN): create the subnets, which are broadcast domains, in **software** instead of by physical wiring. The user plugs into the nearby switch, and the switch is told that this port belongs to subnet 1.'],
    31: ['A **VLAN** groups computers **logically**, by configuration, instead of **physically**, by where they are wired. Here one switch has three VLANs: VLAN 1 on ports 1, 2, 5 and 7; VLAN 2 on ports 3, 4 and 6; VLAN 3 on ports 8, 9 and 10. Each VLAN is a **separate broadcast domain** and a **separate network**: a broadcast from port 1 reaches only ports 2, 5 and 7. The switch keeps a **lookup table** of which port (machine) belongs to which VLAN.'],
    32: ['VLANs aren’t limited to one switch. Here switch A and switch B are joined through a backbone switch, and VLANs 1, 2 and 3 each have members on **both** switches. A broadcast from a VLAN 1 station on switch A reaches the VLAN 1 stations on switch B, but no station in VLAN 2 or 3.'],
    33: ['The one line to remember: **VLANs create broadcast domains**. A switch on its own never splits broadcast domains; a switch with VLANs does, one per VLAN.'],
    34: ['Three advantages. **Lower cost and installation time**: moving a station to another group is done in software instead of rewiring. **More security**: a group that needs high security can be put in its own VLAN, so no one outside can communicate with it directly, and its broadcast messages aren’t received by other VLANs. **Virtual workgroups**: stations in different physical places can be put in the same broadcast domain, for example people from different departments working on one project.'],
}

